INT. PAWNSHOP BACK OFFICE - NIGHT

Fluorescent tube. A steel desk. Shelves of watches, guitars, wedding rings in velvet trays. A safe the size of a refrigerator, door ajar.

BERNICE "BUNNY" OKAFOR-STANWYCK (50s) counts cash into stacks, reading glasses on her nose. Her partner, DELIA VOSS-MBEKI (40s), leans on the desk eating an orange. A boy of twenty, AUGUSTUS "GUS" PELLEGRINI-ADE, sits on a stool by the door, one knee bouncing.

BUNNY
Fifteen thousand. All there.

DELIA
Told you.

GUS
Can I go now?

BUNNY
You're not going anywhere.

GUS
You said, once the count --

DELIA
Gus, honey. Sit.

He sits. He was sitting. He sits harder.

BUNNY
(counting)
Sixteen. Seventeen.

DELIA
You nervous, Gus?

GUS
No.

DELIA
You look nervous.

GUS
It's a pawnshop. I'm fine.

She smiles at him. Peels the orange, a long unbroken spiral. Hands him a segment. He takes it and does not eat it.

BUNNY
Eighteen. Nineteen.

The shop bell rings out front. All three freeze.

DELIA
(quietly)
We're closed.

Through the wall, a truck door slams. Gus goes white.

GUS
(whispering)
That's my dad's truck.

BUNNY
You told us he was in Phoenix.

GUS ^
(whispering)
He was!

DELIA
Bunny.

BUNNY
I know.

Bunny rises and, slowly, takes a small revolver from the drawer. Delia sets the orange peel on the desk. The shop bell rings again.
